# Irregular Periods: Common Causes and What They May Mean

Menstrual cycles do not always follow the exact same schedule every month. Sometimes a period may arrive earlier or later than expected, and occasional changes are not necessarily a sign of a serious health problem.

However, if your periods are consistently irregular or there is a major change from your usual cycle, it is worth paying attention to the pattern.

## What Is an Irregular Period?

An irregular period means that your menstrual cycle does not follow a predictable pattern.

Some people may have periods that come at different intervals, while others may occasionally miss a period or experience changes in the duration and amount of bleeding.

Cycle patterns can naturally differ between individuals.

## Common Causes of Irregular Periods

Hormonal changes are one possible cause.

During the first few years after menstruation begins, the body's reproductive hormones may still be developing, so periods can be unpredictable.

Stress can also influence the menstrual cycle. Major changes in daily routine, emotional stress, lack of sleep, or significant lifestyle changes may sometimes affect when your period arrives.

Changes in body weight, very intense exercise, and certain illnesses can also influence menstrual cycles.

## Hormonal Health Conditions

Some medical conditions can cause irregular periods.

Polycystic ovary syndrome, commonly known as PCOS, is one condition associated with irregular or infrequent menstruation. Thyroid disorders can also affect menstrual cycles.

This does not mean that every irregular period is caused by PCOS or a thyroid problem. A healthcare professional can help determine the possible cause based on your symptoms and health history.

## Pregnancy Can Cause a Missed Period

For people who are sexually active, pregnancy is another possible reason for a missed or delayed period.

If your period is unexpectedly late and pregnancy is possible, taking an appropriate pregnancy test can help clarify the situation.

## Track Your Menstrual Cycle

Tracking your period is one of the easiest ways to understand your cycle.

Record:

* The first day of bleeding
* How many days your period lasts
* Approximate flow
* Pain or cramps
* Spotting between periods
* Other noticeable symptoms

After several months, you may begin to see your usual pattern.

